  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "A billiard ball is projected vertically downward with an initial velocity of $0.25\\,\\text{m/s}$ and hits the ground after $0.025$ minutes. Find the distance the billiard ball covered during its fall. **Do not round your answer**.\n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*\n\nDuring its fall, the billiard ball covered a distance of\n{{field-1}}$\\text{m}.$",
  "worked_solution": "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The initial velocity of the billiard ball is $u=0.25\\,\\text{m/s}.$\n\n- We can find the time it takes for the billiard ball to fall in seconds as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =0.025\\,\\text{min} \\\\  & =0.025\\,\\text{min}\\cdot \\frac{60\\,\\text{s}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =0.025\\,\\cancel{\\text{min}}\\cdot \\frac{60\\,\\text{s}}{\\cancel{\\text{min}}} \\\\  & =1.5\\,\\text{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\  & =(0.25)(1.5)+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8)(1.5)^{2} \\\\  & =0.375+11.025 \\\\  & =11.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $11.4\\,\\text{m}.$",
